. So maybe d4xe5 is not so clear.  \n\nAlternatively, developing pieces is solid. Nf3 (N: g1-f3) develops a knight to a good square, attacks e5, and prepares castling. Bc4 (B: f1-c4) develops the bishop to a strong diagonal.  \n\nGiven that black\'s e5 pawn is advanced and the knight on c6 is loose, playing d4xe5 immediately could disrupt black\'s center. Let\'s check: After d4xe5, if black recaptures with the pawn d7xe5, then white\'s queen on d1 attacks the pawn on e5 and the knight on c6 is still attacked. Black might have to move the knight or defend it, giving white some initiative.  \n\nAnother good move is Nf3, developing and putting pressure on e5.  \n\nConsidering the valid moves, "P: d4-e5" is listed. That is a pawn capture, removing the black pawn on e5 and attacking the knight. This seems the most aggressive and principled move in the opening, as it challenges black\'s center and gains a tempo by attacking the knight.  \n\nFinal choice: Capture on e5 to open the center and create immediate threats.  \n\nAnswer: P: d4-e5'}
